by F. Doray, J. Schepers, B. Servatius, and the author), for more details we refer to the mentioned articles.\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nAvailable via license: CC BY 4.0\n\nContent may be subject to copyright.\n\nArnold Math J.\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.1007/s40598-018-0080-7\n\nPROBLEM CONTRIBUTION\n\nOpen Problems on Conﬁguration Spaces of Tensegrities\n\nOleg Karpenkov 1\n\nReceived: 8 June 2017 / Revised: 13 January 2018 / Accepted: 22 January 2018\n\n© The Author(s) 2018. This article is an open access publication\n\nAbstract In this small paper we bring together some open problems related to the\n\nstudy of the conﬁguration spaces of tensegrities, i.e. graphs with stresses on edges.\n\nThese problems were announced in Doray et al. (Discrete Comput Geom 43:436–466,\n\na non-zero tensegrity in R 2 if and only if six points p 1,p 2,..., p 6 are on a conic (see\n\nFig. 2, Middle). This condition is described via Cayley algebra as Pascal’s theorem:\n\nq 1,q 2 and q 3 areinalinefor ⎧\n\n⎨\n\n⎩\n\nq 1=(p 1∨p 2)∩(p 4∨p 5)\n\nq 2=(p 2∨p 3)∩(p 5∨p 6)\n\nq 3=(p 3∨p 4)∩(p 6∨p 1)\n\n,\n\n(Fig. 2, Right). Similar to Remark 2.1 the set of points of B(K 3,3)that satisfy the above\n\ncondition is the union of several strata for B(K 3,3). The statement of this example is\n\nan exercise on surgeries on graphs introduced in Doray et al. (2010).\n\nIn fact in the above example, the property of 6 points to lie on one conic does not\n\ndepend on the order of these points. Therefore, there are 60 different Cayley algebra\n\nsystems deﬁning the same stratum. This lead to the following important open problem.\n\nProblem 6 Which Cayley algebra systems deﬁne the same strata?\n\nThis problem is a kind of a question on ﬁnding generators and relations for the set\n\nof all conditions.\n\nOne of the main long-standing open problems on the Cayley strata description is\n\nas follows.\n\nProblem 7 Given a graph G. Does there exist a Cayley algebra system (or several\n\nsystems) describing the union of the codimension 1 tensegrity strata in the plane [i.e.,\n\nthe union of the codimension 1 strata of B 2(K n)]?\n\n123\n\nO. Karpenkov\n\nRecently this problem was solved in a weaker settings of extended Cayley algebra\n\nin Karpenkov (2017). Nevertheless it is not clear if it is possible to avoid additional\n\nelements involved in the construction of Karpenkov (2017). Here is an example of a\n\ngraph for which the systems describing codimension 1 strata are not known.\n\nProblem 8 Write (if exist) Cayley algebra systems deﬁning the strata for the following\n\ngraph:\n\nCurrently this example is a strong candidate for a counterexample to Problem 7.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProblem Lists – AMR (https://amathr.org/problems/)\nciteturn1338search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today;   * Karpenkov - Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities (2020)\n  * Klee - Old and new unsolved problems in plane geometry and number theory (1991)\n  * Morgan and Sullivan -Open problems ins soap bubble geometry (1995)\n  * Furuhata, Matsuzo and Urakawa - Open Problems in Affine Differential Geometry (1998)\n  * Aubin - Nonlinear Problems in Riemannian Geometry (1998)\n  * Kapovich - Problems on boundaries of groups and KleinianGroups (2005)\n  * Brass, Moser, Pach - Research Problems in Discrete Geometry (Springer textbook) (2005)\n  * Donaldson - Some problems in differential geometry and topology (2008)\n  * Agrachev - Some open problems in geometric control theory and sub-Riemannian geometry (2013)\n  * Open Problems and Conjectures Related to the Theory of Mathematical Quasicrystals (2016)\n  * Gromov - 101 Questions, Problems and Conjectures around Scalar Curvature. (Incomplete and Unedited Version) (2017)\n  * Morgan and Pansu - A list of open problems in Differential Geometry (2018)\n  * Karpenkov - Open Problems on Configuration Spaces of Tensegrities (2020)\n  * Ghomi - Open problems in geometry of curves and surfaces (2019)\n  * Gromov - Still unsolved problems - video lecture (2021)\n  * Geometry problems from Wikipedia\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOleg Karpenkov - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/author/oleg-karpenkov)\nciteturn1338search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Oleg Karpenkov, Jan Schepers, Brigitte Servatius ... Within this paper we formulate additional open problems related to the stratification space of tensegrities. ... Geometry of configuration spaces of tensegrities\ntree\n\n2012-01-17\n\nOleg Karpenkov, Jan Schepers, Brigitte Servatius\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nOn stratifications for planar tensegrities with a small number of vertices\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nIn this paper we discuss several results about the structure of the configuration space of two-dimensional tensegrities with a small number of points. We briefly describe the technique of surgeries that is used to find geometric conditions for tensegrities. Further we introduce a new surgery for three-dimensional tensegrities. Within this paper we formulate additional open problems related to the stratification space of tensegrities.\n\nconfiguration space planar\n\n2008-06-30 v2\n\nFranck Doray, Oleg Karpenkov, Jan Schepers\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nGeometry of configuration spaces of tensegrities\n\nPublished • View Publication • BIB\n\nConsider a graph G with n vertices. In this paper we study geometric conditions for an n-tuple of points in R^d to admit a tensegrity with underlying graph G. We introduce and investigate a natural stratification, depending on G, of the configuration space of all n-tuples in R^d. In particular we find surgeries on graphs that give relations between different strata. Based on numerous examples we give a description of geometric conditions defining the strata for plane tensegrities, we conjecture that the list of such conditions is sufficient to describe any stratum. We conclude the paper with particular examples of strata for tensegrities in the plane with a small number of vertices.\n\nconfiguration space graph natural\n\n2006-11-30\n\nOleg Karpenkov\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2-universality in randomly perturbed graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.01823)\nciteturn1338academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.8 years ago; Title: 2-universality in randomly perturbed graphs ... Furthermore, this extends a result of B\\\"ottcher, Montgomery, Parczyk, and Person [Embedding spanning bounded degree subgraphs in randomly perturbed graphs, arXiv:1802.04603 (2018)], who embed a given $F \\in \\mathcal{F}(n,2)$ at these values.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommunication Complexity (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=e76af992d981315222f78de02f96a20d9564faba&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn1338search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; arXiv:0912.1777v4  [cs.DM]  16 Sep 2010 ... automata (CA) has received a lot of attention (see [12] for a survey). ... universality was proposed: A CA is intrinsically universal if it is able to simu- ... This deﬁnition of intrinsic universality may seem very restrictive. ... Preprint submitted to Elsevier\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUniversality for mathematical and physical systems (https://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0603038)\nciteturn1338academia15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.7 years ago; In other words, whatever the precise nature of the interaction between the atoms and molecules at the microscopic level, at the macroscopic level, physical systems exhibit universal behavior in the sense that they are